---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import Nav from "../components/Nav.astro";
import Footer from "../components/Footer.astro";
import InstallCommand from "../components/InstallCommand.astro";
import CodeBlock from "../components/docs/CodeBlock.astro";
import Callout from "../components/docs/Callout.astro";
import CommandUsage from "../components/docs/CommandUsage.astro";
import { commands } from "../data/commands";

// The "On this page" list. Keep the ids in step with the h2s below.
const toc = [
  ["install", "Install"],
  ["setup", "Guided setup"],
  ["how-it-works", "How it works"],
  ["accounts", "Accounts"],
  ["handoff", "Handoff"],
  ["agents", "Agents"],
  ["commands", "Commands"],
  ["troubleshooting", "Troubleshooting"],
  ["privacy", "Privacy and safety"],
  ["uninstall", "Uninstall"],
];
---

<Base
  title="Handova — Documentation"
  description="Install Handova, set up accounts for Claude Code and Codex, and hand a conversation from one account to another. Every command and option, explained."
>
  <Nav />
  <main class="mx-auto max-w-6xl px-4 pb-28 pt-12 sm:px-8 sm:pt-16 lg:grid lg:grid-cols-[13rem_minmax(0,1fr)] lg:gap-16">
    <aside class="lg:sticky lg:top-24 lg:self-start" aria-label="On this page">
      <details class="group rounded-xl border border-line bg-surface lg:hidden">
        <summary class="flex cursor-pointer list-none items-center justify-between px-4 py-3 text-[0.9rem] text-ink-soft [&::-webkit-details-marker]:hidden">
          On this page
          <span class="text-faint transition-transform group-open:rotate-90" aria-hidden="true">›</span>
        </summary>
        <nav class="border-t border-line px-2 py-2">
          {toc.map(([id, label]) => (
            <a href={`#${id}`} class="block rounded-md px-2 py-1.5 text-[0.88rem] text-muted hover:text-ink">{label}</a>
          ))}
        </nav>
      </details>
      <nav class="hidden lg:block">
        <p class="font-mono text-[0.68rem] uppercase tracking-[0.14em] text-faint">On this page</p>
        <ul class="mt-4 space-y-0.5 border-l border-line">
          {toc.map(([id, label]) => (
            <li>
              <a href={`#${id}`} class="toc-link -ml-px block border-l border-transparent py-1.5 pl-4 text-[0.88rem] text-muted transition-colors hover:text-ink" data-toc={id}>{label}</a>
            </li>
          ))}
        </ul>
      </nav>
    </aside>

    <article class="docs-prose mt-10 max-w-[44rem] lg:mt-0">
      <h1 class="text-[2.6rem] font-semibold leading-[1] tracking-[-0.05em] text-ink sm:text-[3.4rem]">
        Getting <span class="text-muted">started.</span>
      </h1>
      <p class="!mt-5 text-[1.08rem]">
        Install Handova, let the guided setup add your accounts, then hand a conversation to another account whenever one
        hits its usage limit. Handova works with Claude Code and Codex.
      </p>

      <h2 id="install">Install</h2>
      <p>Install it globally with your package manager:</p>
      <div class="block max-w-md"><InstallCommand /></div>
      <p>
        Yarn 2 and later have no global installs, so use Yarn 1 or another manager. To try it once without installing, run
        <code>npx handova</code>, <code>pnpm dlx handova</code> or <code>bunx handova</code>.
      </p>
      <p><strong>You'll need:</strong></p>
      <ul>
        <li>Claude Code, Codex or both, installed and working on their own first.</li>
        <li>Node.js 20 or newer, or Bun.</li>
        <li>macOS. Linux should work; Windows isn't supported yet.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2 id="setup">Guided setup</h2>
      <p>Once it's installed, run:</p>
      <CodeBlock code="handova setup" />
      <p>Setup walks you through everything, one question at a time:</p>
      <ol>
        <li>
          <strong>Which agents.</strong> If both Claude Code and Codex are installed, it asks whether you want more than one
          account for Claude Code, Codex or both. Agents you haven't installed aren't offered. With only one installed, it skips
          the question.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Your normal login.</strong> For each agent it shows the login you already have (<code>personal</code> for
          Claude Code, <code>codex</code> for Codex) and offers to log in if it isn't yet.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>More accounts.</strong> It asks whether to add another account, suggests a name, creates it with your
          instructions and skills shared, and opens the agent's own login. It keeps asking until you say no.
        </li>
        <li><strong>Summary.</strong> It lists your accounts and shows the commands to try next.</li>
      </ol>
      <p>
        Run it again any time to add more accounts. It keeps everything you already have, and if you stop halfway, whatever
        was added stays.
      </p>
      <Callout title="Prefer commands?">
        <p>Setup is optional. Everything it does is also a command: <code>handova add</code>, <code>handova list</code> and so on. See <a href="#commands">Commands</a>.</p>
      </Callout>

      <h2 id="how-it-works">How it works</h2>
      <p>
        Each coding agent keeps an account's login, settings and conversations in a config folder, and lets you point it at a
        different folder with an environment variable. Handova gives every account its own folder and sets that variable when it
        starts the agent.
      </p>
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr><th>Agent</th><th>Normal folder</th><th>Extra accounts</th><th>Set with</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>Claude Code</td><td><code>~/.claude</code></td><td><code>~/.claude-&lt;name&gt;</code></td><td><code>CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR</code></td></tr>
          <tr><td>Codex</td><td><code>~/.codex</code></td><td><code>~/.codex-&lt;name&gt;</code></td><td><code>CODEX_HOME</code></td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
      <p>
        Because every account has its own folder, the logins are separate. Logging in or out of one account never affects
        another, and you can run several at once in different terminals.
      </p>
      <p>
        Handova keeps its list of accounts in <code>~/.config/handova/accounts.json</code>. Until something is saved there, it
        shows the normal login of each agent you have installed.
      </p>

      <h2 id="accounts">Accounts</h2>
      <p>
        An account is one of your logins for one agent. Each account remembers its agent, so after you add it you only use its
        name: <code>handova work</code> starts Claude Code if <code>work</code> is a Claude Code account, and Codex if it's a
        Codex account. The name is only a label.
      </p>

      <h3 id="normal-logins">Normal logins</h3>
      <p>
        The first time you run Handova, it lists the login you already have for each installed agent: <code>personal</code> for
        Claude Code and <code>codex</code> for Codex. They're marked <strong>(default)</strong> in <code>handova list</code> and
        run the agent exactly as it runs on its own. Rename them if you like, or take one off the list if you don't use that
        agent.
      </p>

      <h3 id="adding">Adding an account</h3>
      <CodeBlock code={`handova add work                    # a Claude Code account
handova add codex-work --agent codex # a Codex account`} />
      <p>
        A new account gets its own folder and opens the agent's login. Your instructions follow it: these are linked from your
        normal login, so edits show up in every account, while settings are copied so each account can change its own.
      </p>
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th>Agent</th><th>Shared (linked)</th><th>Copied</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>Claude Code</td><td><code>CLAUDE.md</code>, <code>skills</code>, <code>agents</code>, <code>commands</code></td><td><code>settings.json</code></td></tr>
          <tr><td>Codex</td><td><code>AGENTS.md</code>, <code>prompts</code>, <code>skills</code></td><td><code>config.toml</code></td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
      <p>
        Use <code>--dir</code> to adopt a folder you already have; if it's logged in, the login step is skipped. Pointing it at an
        agent's normal folder registers that login, for example if you installed Codex after you started using Handova:
      </p>
      <CodeBlock code="handova add codex --agent codex --dir ~/.codex" />
      <p>Names use lowercase letters, digits and dashes.</p>

      <h3 id="renaming">Renaming and removing</h3>
      <CodeBlock code={`handova rename work office   # only the name changes
handova remove office         # forget it; folder and login stay
handova remove office --purge # also log out and delete its folder`} />
      <p>
        <code>--purge</code> asks first, and never deletes an agent's normal folder: removing <code>personal</code> or
        <code>codex</code> only takes them off Handova's list.
      </p>

      <h2 id="handoff">Handoff</h2>
      <p>
        A handoff moves a conversation from one of your accounts to another, so you can keep going when one hits its usage
        limit. <strong>Both accounts must be the same agent.</strong>
      </p>
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th>From</th><th>To</th><th>Works?</th><th>What moves</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>Claude Code</td><td>Claude Code</td><td>Yes</td><td>The conversation, files saved with it (like subagent transcripts) and that folder's memory</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Codex</td><td>Codex</td><td>Yes</td><td>The conversation. Codex keeps memory per account, not per folder, so it stays</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Claude Code</td><td>Codex, or back</td><td>Not yet</td><td>Nothing. Handova stops before copying and explains why</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
      <CodeBlock code={`handova handoff personal work     # Claude Code to Claude Code
handova handoff codex codex-work  # Codex to Codex
handova handoff work personal     # and back again`} />

      <h3 id="handoff-steps">What happens</h3>
      <ol>
        <li><strong>Run it where you were working.</strong> Conversations are kept per folder, so run it in the folder you used the agent in.</li>
        <li><strong>It finds the conversation:</strong> your latest one in that folder, or the one you pick with <code>--pick</code> or <code>--session &lt;id&gt;</code>.</li>
        <li><strong>It asks first.</strong> It names the account and organization the conversation is going to, and waits for a yes. Skip the question with <code>--yes</code>.</li>
        <li><strong>It copies, never overwriting.</strong> Anything already on the other account is kept.</li>
        <li><strong>It opens it there.</strong> The agent starts on the other account with the conversation loaded. Use <code>--no-resume</code> to copy only.</li>
      </ol>

      <h3 id="handoff-rules">The fine print</h3>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>The copy is a fork.</strong> What you do on the new account doesn't flow back to the old one.</li>
        <li><strong>Handing over again</strong> brings an older copy up to date, if you only carried on with the original.</li>
        <li><strong>If both copies were continued,</strong> they've diverged. Handova won't merge them and tells you to resume on the other account instead.</li>
        <li><strong>Claude Code memory</strong> merges file by file: files the other account already has are kept and listed, and missing lines are added to <code>MEMORY.md</code>. Leave memory behind with <code>--no-memory</code>.</li>
        <li><strong>Not copied:</strong> the undo history for file edits, so rewind can't reach before the handoff.</li>
      </ul>
      <Callout title="Organizations">
        <p>
          Handing a conversation to another account means it's processed under that account's organization. Handova names it
          and asks before copying. If your workplace doesn't allow moving work between accounts, don't.
        </p>
      </Callout>

      <h2 id="agents">Agents</h2>
      <h3 id="claude-code">Claude Code</h3>
      <ul>
        <li>Logins are checked with <code>claude auth status</code> and opened with <code>claude auth login</code>. <code>handova list</code> shows the email and organization.</li>
        <li>Conversations live in <code>projects/</code> inside the account's folder, grouped by the folder you started Claude Code in.</li>
        <li>Memory is per folder, so a handoff can bring it along.</li>
        <li>Your normal login runs with <code>CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR</code> unset, exactly like plain <code>claude</code>.</li>
      </ul>
      <h3 id="codex">Codex</h3>
      <ul>
        <li>Logins are checked with <code>codex login status</code> and opened with <code>codex login</code>. For a ChatGPT login, <code>handova list</code> shows the email and plan (for example "ChatGPT plus") in the organization column; for an API key, "API key".</li>
        <li>Conversations live in <code>sessions/</code> inside the account's folder, filed by date. A handoff copies the conversation to the same place in the other account, and <code>codex resume</code> picks it up with its full history.</li>
        <li>Memory is per account, so it isn't moved.</li>
        <li>Your normal login runs with <code>CODEX_HOME</code> unset, exactly like plain <code>codex</code>.</li>
      </ul>
      <h3 id="adding-agents">Adding more agents</h3>
      <p>
        Everything specific to an agent lives in one file in <code>src/harness/</code>. Before an agent is added, its login and
        conversation storage are checked against a real install. The checklist is in the
        <a href="https://github.com/DeveloperAspire/handova/blob/master/CLAUDE.md#adding-a-harness">project's CLAUDE.md</a>.
      </p>

      <h2 id="commands">Commands</h2>
      <p>Every command, part by part.</p>
      {commands.map((command) => (
        <>
          <h3 id={`cmd-${command.id}`}>{command.title}</h3>
          <CommandUsage parts={command.parts} />
          <p>{command.body}</p>
          {command.options && (
            <table>
              <tbody>
                {command.options.map(([flag, meaning]) => (
                  <tr><td class="whitespace-nowrap"><code>{flag}</code></td><td>{meaning}</td></tr>
                ))}
              </tbody>
            </table>
          )}
        </>
      ))}
      <p>
        <code>handova help</code> lists every command and <code>handova --version</code> prints the installed version.
      </p>

      <h2 id="troubleshooting">Troubleshooting</h2>
      <p>Start with:</p>
      <CodeBlock code="handova doctor" caption="Checks each agent is installed, every account is logged in and the shared links still work, and says how to fix anything that isn't." />
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th>You see</th><th>What to do</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td><code>can't run "codex"</code> in the list</td><td>That agent isn't installed or isn't on your PATH. Install it, or take the account off the list.</td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>not logged in</code></td><td>Log in with <code>handova &lt;account&gt; auth login</code> for Claude Code, or <code>handova &lt;account&gt; login</code> for Codex.</td></tr>
          <tr><td>No sessions for this folder</td><td>Run the handoff from the folder you started the agent in.</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Already further along on the other account</td><td>That account has the newer copy. Resume it there with the command Handova prints.</td></tr>
          <tr><td>The two copies have diverged</td><td>Both were continued. Handova won't merge them; resume on the account you want to keep.</td></tr>
          <tr><td>A Claude Code account and a Codex account</td><td>Handoffs only work between accounts of the same agent.</td></tr>
          <tr><td>This needs a confirmation</td><td>You're not in an interactive terminal. Run it again with <code>--yes</code>.</td></tr>
          <tr><td>No accounts yet</td><td>Install Claude Code or Codex, then run <code>handova setup</code>.</td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR</code> or <code>CODEX_HOME</code> is set in your shell</td><td>Remove it from your shell profile. Handova sets it per account.</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>

      <h2 id="privacy">Privacy and safety</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>No network requests.</strong> Handova copies files on your computer and starts your agent, which talks to its provider as it always does.</li>
        <li><strong>Logins stay with the agents.</strong> Handova never stores logins. It asks each agent whether it's logged in, and for Codex reads the email and plan from Codex's own login file to show in the list.</li>
        <li><strong>It confirms before it sends or deletes.</strong> Handoffs and <code>--purge</code> both ask first.</li>
        <li><strong>It never deletes your normal folders</strong> (<code>~/.claude</code>, <code>~/.codex</code>), and never edits your shell profile.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2 id="uninstall">Uninstall</h2>
      <CodeBlock code={`handova remove work --purge   # optional: delete extra accounts first
npm uninstall -g handova      # or: pnpm remove -g, yarn global remove, bun remove -g`} />
      <p>
        Your normal Claude Code and Codex logins aren't touched. To forget Handova's account list too, delete
        <code>~/.config/handova</code>.
      </p>
    </article>
  </main>
  <Footer />
</Base>

<style is:global>
  .toc-link.is-active {
    border-color: var(--color-ink);
    color: var(--color-ink);
  }
</style>

<script>
  // Highlight the section you're reading in the "On this page" list.
  const links = new Map(
    [...document.querySelectorAll<HTMLAnchorElement>("[data-toc]")].map((link) => [link.dataset.toc!, link]),
  );
  const headings = [...links.keys()].map((id) => document.getElementById(id)).filter((el): el is HTMLElement => !!el);

  function update() {
    const current = headings.filter((h) => h.getBoundingClientRect().top < 140).at(-1) ?? headings[0];
    links.forEach((link, id) => link.classList.toggle("is-active", id === current?.id));
  }
  addEventListener("scroll", update, { passive: true });
  update();
</script>
